  "dom_id": "step-q8075",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Write the complex number $z=1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "To write $z$ in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{2}{1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(2) \\\\  & \u22481.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =1.107$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{1.107\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$",
